, and full post-restart recovery of certificate files, SSH, nginx, and TLS/CA trust. Operational hygiene is strong; a handful of openssl command-line syntax failures due to the older openssl version on Ubuntu 16.04 were quickly self-corrected without leaving conflicting or damaged state.\",\"limitations\":[\"Node2's package-health and listening-socket global observations in the fixed snapshots return generic/empty output, so cross-verification of nginx package state relies on the executor's own dpkg/ss commands rather than the fixed collector snapshot.\",\"The before-prepare and after-prepare snapshots are identical (prepare phase disabled/aliased), so no preparer-created state could be distinguished from executor changes; this does not affect confidence here since no unexpected residue was found.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"ca-created","requirement":"Create a certificate authority named 'Fleet Internal CA' on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"CA private key (4096-bit RSA) and self-signed x509 CA certificate generated on node1 with subject=CN=Fleet Internal CA,O=Fleet Internal CA, 10-year validity (2026-2036). Confirmed via direct openssl x509 -text inspection.","evidence_ids":["cmd-74ec67f109da44d4","cmd-e70b5c87a05845d7","cmd-72dbba5f66e44fe0"]},{"id":"cert-issued","requirement":"Issue node2 a TLS certificate signed by the CA","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2 key/CSR generated with CN=node2 and SAN (DNS:node2, IP:10.100.160.11); signed by the CA producing certs/node2.cert.pem with 'Signature ok' and openssl verify 'OK'. Final cert inspection confirms issuer=CN=Fleet Internal CA,O=Fleet Internal CA and correct SAN.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ed11bccee5fc4a69","cmd-51984333b214498b","cmd-2181bb77422046d3"]},{"id":"https-8443","requirement":"Configure node2 to serve HTTPS on port 8443 using the issued certificate","status":"satisfied","summary":"nginx installed and configured on node2 with a vhost listening on 8443 using the deployed cert+ke